{"acceptance_length": 2.507042253521127, "acceptance_lengths": [3, 1, 3, 4, 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 1, 3, 3, 1, 1, 3, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 1, 3, 4, 5, 1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 2, 1, 5, 2, 4, 2, 6, 3, 1, 1, 1, 1, 5, 1, 3, 3, 7, 2, 5, 3, 3, 3, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 3, 1, 1, 4, 3, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 5, 3, 2, 2, 2, 1, 3, 1, 3, 5, 1, 3, 1, 8, 1, 3, 1, 4, 3, 1, 3, 1, 1, 1, 1, 4, 1, 2, 1, 1, 1, 3, 1, 1, 3, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 3, 1, 8, 1, 3, 1, 8, 2, 2, 1, 7, 1, 3, 1, 4, 1, 3, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 2, 1, 1, 3, 1, 14, 9, 4, 1, 3, 1, 1, 1, 5, 1, 1, 3, 1, 4, 1, 16, 9, 3, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1, 9, 1, 6, 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 1, 3, 5, 3, 1, 4, 2, 1, 5, 1, 1, 5, 1, 6, 2, 3, 1, 1, 6, 10, 10, 5, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 3, 1, 3, 1, 4, 2, 3, 1, 3, 1], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 34409770496, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 34409764864, "benchmark": "math500", "completion": "To find the minimal compound interest rate, we need to calculate the total amount in the account after three years and then find the interest rate that will give us at least $\\$66{,}200$.\n\nLet's denote the interest rate as $r$ (in decimal form, so $r = 0.05$ for $5\\%$). The formula for compound interest is:\n\n\\[A = P(1 + r)^n\\]\n\nwhere $A$ is the amount after $n$ years, $P$ is the principal amount (initial deposit), and $r$ is the interest rate.\n\nIn this case, the worker deposits $\\$20{,}000$ at the end of each year, so we have three deposits. We can calculate the total amount after three years as follows:\n\n\\[A = 20{,}000(1 + r)^3 + 20{,}000(1 + r)^2 + 20{,}000(1 + r) + 20{,}000\\]\n\nWe want to find the minimal interest rate that will give us at least $\\$66{,}200$, so we set up the inequality:\n\n\\[20{,}000(1 + r)^3 + 20{,}000(1 + r)^2 + 20{,}000(1 + r) + 20{,}000 \\geq 66{,}200\\]\n\nSimplifying the inequality, we get:\n\n\\[20{,}000((1 + r)^3 + (1 + r)^2 + (1 + r) + 1) \\geq 66{,}200\\]\n\nDividing both sides by $20{,}000$, we get:\n\n\\[(1 + r)^3 + (1 + r)^2 + (1 + r) + 1 \\geq 3.3100\\]\n\nNow, we can try different values of $r$ to find the minimal interest rate that satisfies the inequality. We can start with a small value of $r$, such as $r = 0.01$, and increase it until we find the smallest value that satisfies the inequality.\n\nAfter trying different values, we find that $r = 0.06$ satisfies the inequality:\n\n\\[(1 + 0.06)^3 + (1 + 0.06)^2 + (1 + 0.06) + 1 = 3.3103\\]\n\nTherefore, the minimal compound interest rate that the savings account must provide is $\\boxed{6.0}$%.", "decode_seconds": 13.058254681061953, "draft_calls": 213, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 147, "method": "relay_p_cross_family", "output_hash": "f11e5f7c640945ceb1b7d9de8fa6cf7303e026fd4c79a059d9fb278849092861", "output_token_ids": [1271, 1505, 279, 17832, 24549, 2802, 4478, 11, 584, 1205, 311, 11294, 279, 2860, 3392, 304, 279, 2759, 1306, 2380, 1667, 323, 1243, 1505, 279, 2802, 4478, 430, 690, 3041, 603, 520, 3325, 59060, 3, 2287, 90, 11, 92, 1049, 3, 382, 10267, 596, 79164, 279, 2802, 4478, 439, 400, 81, 3, 320, 258, 12395, 1376, 11, 779, 400, 81, 284, 220, 15, 13, 2304, 3, 369, 400, 20, 59, 4, 3, 570, 578, 15150, 369, 24549, 2802, 374, 1473, 79145, 32, 284, 393, 7, 16, 489, 436, 30876, 77, 59, 2595, 2940, 400, 32, 3, 374, 279, 3392, 1306, 400, 77, 3, 1667, 11, 400, 47, 3, 374, 279, 12717, 3392, 320, 9613, 16946, 705, 323, 400, 81, 3, 374, 279, 2802, 4478, 382, 644, 420, 1162, 11, 279, 12128, 34751, 59060, 3, 508, 90, 11, 92, 931, 3, 520, 279, 842, 315, 1855, 1060, 11, 779, 584, 617, 2380, 34751, 13, 1226, 649, 11294, 279, 2860, 3392, 1306, 2380, 1667, 439, 11263, 1473, 79145, 32, 284, 220, 508, 90, 11, 92, 931, 7, 16, 489, 436, 30876, 18, 489, 220, 508, 90, 11, 92, 931, 7, 16, 489, 436, 30876, 17, 489, 220, 508, 90, 11, 92, 931, 7, 16, 489, 436, 8, 489, 220, 508, 90, 11, 92, 931, 59, 2595, 1687, 1390, 311, 1505, 279, 17832, 2802, 4478, 430, 690, 3041, 603, 520, 3325, 59060, 3, 2287, 90, 11, 92, 1049, 55976, 779, 584, 743, 709, 279, 32305, 1473, 79145, 508, 90, 11, 92, 931, 7, 16, 489, 436, 30876